Sign in with any OIDC provider inside Dekart, with server-side sessions

`DEKART_REQUIRE_OIDC` trusts a JWT that a reverse proxy puts in
`X-Forwarded-Access-Token` and never reads `Authorization`. Device tokens
(`dekart init`, the GeoSQL MCP server) and snapshot tokens arrive as bearers, so
they get 401, and the proxy in front refuses even `POST /api/v1/device` because
the CLI carries no proxy cookie.

`DEKART_REQUIRE_OIDC_LOGIN` makes Dekart the OIDC client: the authorization code
flow with PKCE, state and nonce runs at `/api/v1/oidc/{login,callback,logout}`
against endpoints from discovery, and the ID token is checked by the existing
`oidcJWTVerifier`. The browser gets an opaque HttpOnly `dekart_session` cookie;
the row in `oidc_sessions` stores its hash and the provider refresh token
encrypted with `DEKART_OIDC_TOKEN_KEY`. When the provider access token expires
the server refreshes it, and a refused refresh deletes the session, so a user
disabled at the provider loses access. Bearers are tried first and never fall
back to the cookie: snapshot, then device, as Amazon mode does.

This patch carries the wiring only: config fields and their env reads, the mode
in the `b2i` exclusion and `enabledSSOEnvVars()`, the `GetContext` branch, and
the routes. The flow, the session store and the migration are overlay files.

Covered by `oidclogin_test.go`.
